The Next.js App Router brought a lot of architectural improvements, but writing raw API Route Handlers (app/api/**/route.ts) still feels remarkably repetitive.
Every time you write an endpoint, you end up repeating the exact same ceremonies:
- Wrapping everything in try/catch.
- Extracting and parsing JSON bodies with await request.json().
- Running schema validation (like Zod) and manually structuring a 400 response.
- Manually unwrapping asynchronous dynamic parameters in Next.js 15 (await context.params).
- Writing repetitive authentication checks and duplicating TypeScript types.
I got tired of pasting the same 35 lines of setup code for a 3-line database operation. So I built next-routed — a lightweight, zero-dependency, type-safe fluent pipeline for Next.js route handlers.
The Problem: The Native Route Handler Dance
Here is what a standard, validated Next.js 15 route handler typically looks like:
import { NextResponse, type NextRequest } from "next/server";
import { z } from "zod";
import { db } from "@/lib/db";
import { verifyAuth } from "@/lib/auth";
const postSchema = z.object({
  title: "z.string().min(3),"
  content: z.string(),
});
export async function POST(
  request: NextRequest,
  context: { params: Promise<{ orgId: string }> }
) {
  try {
    // 1. Auth check
    const user = await verifyAuth(request);
    if (!user) {
      return NextResponse.json({ id: "UNAUTHORIZED", data: null }, { status: 401 });
    }
    // 2. Unwrapping Next.js 15 params
    const { orgId } = await context.params;
    // 3. Body parsing & validation
    const rawBody = await request.json().catch(() => ({}));
    const parsed = postSchema.safeParse(rawBody);
    if (!parsed.success) {
      return NextResponse.json(
        { id: "VALIDATION_ERROR", errors: parsed.error.issues },
        { status: 400 }
      );
    }
    // 4. Actual business logic
    const post = await db.post.create({
      data: { ...parsed.data, orgId, authorId: user.id },
    });
    return NextResponse.json({ id: "SUCCESS", data: post }, { status: 201 });
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    return NextResponse.json({ id: "INTERNAL_ERROR", data: null }, { status: 500 });
  }
}
Most of this file is transport mechanics and defensive boilerplate. The actual business logic is completely drowned out.
The Solution: A Fluent, Inferred Pipeline
With next-routed, you define your route as a composable pipeline. Types are inferred automatically from your schemas and middlewares — no manual type definitions required.
1. Installation
npm install next-routed
# or
pnpm add next-routed
(Works with Zod or any validator adhering to the Standard Schema v1 specification).


  2. The Same Route with next-routed
First, set up your base reusable auth middleware in lib/api.ts:
// lib/api.ts
import { route, RouteResponse, HttpStatus } from "next-routed";
import { verifyAuth } from "./auth";
export const authedRoute = route.use(async ({ request }) => {
  const user = await verifyAuth(request);
  if (!user) {
    return RouteResponse.status(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED).json(
      { message: "Authentication required" },
      "UNAUTHORIZED"
    );
  }
  // Merged into the route context downstream
  return { user };
});
Now, write your actual endpoint:
// app/api/orgs/[orgId]/posts/route.ts
import { authedRoute } from "@/lib/api";
import { RouteResponse, HttpStatus } from "next-routed";
import { z } from "zod";
import { db } from "@/lib/db";
export const POST = authedRoute
  .params(z.object({ orgId: z.string() }))
  .body(z.object({ title: z.string().min(3), content: z.string() }))
  .handle(async ({ user, params, body }) => {
    // - user is guaranteed and typed
    // - params.orgId is unwrapped and strictly typed
    // - body.title is typed as string
    const post = await db.post.create({
      data: { ...body, orgId: params.orgId, authorId: user.id },
    });
    return RouteResponse.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).json(post, "POST_CREATED");
  });
Why this approach?
1. Zero Manual TypeScript Annotations
You don't need to define type Context = ... or use z.infer. The chain automatically infers every input (body, query, params, and context from .use()) and presents them in handle().
2. Next.js 14 and 15 Compatibility
Next.js 15 made route params asynchronous (Promise<Params>). next-routed unwraps them under the hood, regardless of whether you're targeting Next 14 or 15.


  3. Predictable HTTP Responses with RouteResponse
Instead of manually configuring NextResponse, you get a fluent builder for status codes, headers, cookies, and formats:
return RouteResponse.status(HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT)
  .cookie("session", "", { maxAge: 0 })
  .empty();
If you don't need custom headers or status codes, simply return an object from .handle() — it will automatically be wrapped into a standardized { id: "SUCCESS", data } payload with status 200.
Try it out & Feedback Welcome
next-routed is fully open-source and has zero runtime dependencies beyond Next.js itself.
- 📦 npm: npmjs.com/package/next-routed
- 🐙 GitHub: github.com/slxca/next-routed
If you're building APIs with the App Router, give it a try and let me know your thoughts or feature requests in the comments!

Who said learning AI System Design has to be all dark cyberpunk terminal screens and exploding servers?
To make Production AI Agent Architecture intuitive, visual, and genuinely fun, I built and open-sourced Prompt Paws: The Cozy AI Agent Cafe & Bakery — a 60 FPS playable browser game where adorable Robot-Cat Pastry Chefs teach you LLM Routing, Context Caching, Input Guardrails, RAG Grounding, Deterministic Tool Calling, System-2 Extended Thinking, and Multi-Agent Orchestration!
Play It Live in Your Browser!
- Live Playable Game (60 FPS HTML5 Canvas): https://robinamirbahar.github.io/cozy-ai-agent-cafe-game/
- Open-Source GitHub Repository: https://github.com/RobinaMirbahar/cozy-ai-agent-cafe-game
Pro Tip: Want to see a 5-Star Reference AI Pipeline immediately? Click the purple Auto-Chef Blueprint button in the top bar on any Day (1 to 7) and watch the Robot-Cat Chefs orchestrate the entire bakery automatically!
1. Why Gamify AI Agent Architecture as a Cozy Bakery?
When engineers design production LLM applications, most outages and cost explosions come down to five architectural anti-patterns:
- Unfiltered Prompt Injections: Passing untrusted user input directly into a privileged model or tool without an input guardrail sieve.
- Uncached Prefix Waste: Re-sending the same 100k-token system prompt and documentation on every single turn instead of using Context Caching (up to 90–95% discount).
- Ungrounded Hallucinations: Asking an LLM to recall volatile or private facts from weights instead of grounding it via RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation).
- Probabilistic Math: Asking a token predictor to do arithmetic or guess API state instead of equipping it with Deterministic Function Calling / Code Execution.
- Single-Model Bottlenecks: Routing every trivial request to a slow, expensive reasoning model instead of combining Fast Flash Models, System-2 Thinking Models, and Parallel Subagents.
In Prompt Paws, woodland critters line up at your cafe counter ordering six distinct AI treats—and every kitchen station maps 1-to-1 to a real engineering layer:
| Cozy Bakery Station | Critter Order Handled | Production AI Engineering Layer | 
|---|---|---|
| Flash Pastry Oven | Prompt Croissant | Fast Low-Latency Model ( gemini-2.5-flash) | 
| Context Cache Jar | Repeat Owl Menu Query | Prefix & Context Caching (95% Token Savings) | 
| Guardrail Flour Sieve | Spicy Injection Jalapeno | Input Classifier & Prompt-Injection Sanitizer | 
| Vector Berry Patch | RAG Berry Tart | Vector Search & Fact Grounding (Anti-Hallucination) | 
| Tool-Call Boba Mixer | Code-Exec Boba Tea | Deterministic Python Sandbox & Function Calling | 
| Deep-Think Souffle Oven | Reasoning Souffle | Extended Thinking / System-2 Deliberation | 
| Subagent Sous-Chef Bell | Parallel Banquet Aura | Multi-Agent Parallel Delegation (+45% Speed Aura) | 


  2. Inside the 60 FPS Kawaii Simulation Engine (game.js)
The entire game runs at 60 FPS using pure HTML5 2D Canvas and ES6 JavaScript—with zero external dependencies and zero build step.
Routing Critter Orders to the Right AI Station
Each station inspects passing orders on the winding bakery conveyor ribbon and only picks up tasks that match its architectural role. Notice how Spicy Prompt-Injection Jalapenos cannot be safely baked by normal ovens until a Guardrail Flour Sieve sanitizes them into safe croissants:
function canStationProcessOrder(stationType, ord) {
  // 1. Only the Guardrail Flour Sieve can catch and sanitize Spicy Prompt Injections!
  if (stationType === 'guardrail_sieve') {
    return ord.kind === 'jalapeno';
  }
  // Unfiltered prompt injections bypass normal ovens and damage Cafe Reputation Hearts!
  if (ord.kind === 'jalapeno') {
    return false;
  }
  // 2. Context Cache Jar serves repeated menu queries at 3x speed + bonus Star Coins
  if (stationType === 'cookie_cache') {
    return ord.kind === 'cached' || ord.kind === 'croissant';
  }
  // 3. Vector Berry Patch grounds Berry Tarts so they don't become Hallucination Puffs
  if (stationType === 'vector_garden') {
    return ord.kind === 'tart';
  }
  // 4. Tool-Call Boba Mixer runs deterministic calculations & API recipes
  if (stationType === 'tool_blender') {
    return ord.kind === 'boba';
  }
  // 5. Deep-Think Souffle Oven handles multi-step System-2 reasoning
  if (stationType === 'reasoning_oven') {
    return ord.kind === 'souffle' || ord.kind === 'croissant';
  }
  // 6. Flash Pastry Oven handles high-throughput everyday Prompt Croissants
  if (stationType === 'flash_oven') {
    return ord.kind === 'croissant' || ord.kind === 'cached';
  }
  // 7. Subagent Sous-Chef Bell orchestrates parallel helpers
  if (stationType === 'subagent_bell') {
    return ord.kind !== 'jalapeno';
  }
  return false;
}
3. Real-Time AI Architecture Linter ("Chef Whiskers")
Before you even press Open Cafe, Chef Whiskers (Staff AI Pastry Architect) performs live static analysis on your placed stations and compares them against the upcoming shift's traffic distribution:
function evaluateKitchenArchitecture() {
  const lvl = window.CAFE_LEVELS[currentLevelIdx];
  const weights = lvl.orderWeights;
  const typesPlaced = Object.values(placedStations).map((s) => s.type);
  const alerts = [];
  if (weights.jalapeno && !typesPlaced.includes('guardrail_sieve')) {
    alerts.push({
      kind: 'warn',
      text: 'Prompt Injection Risk! Place a Guardrail Flour Sieve near Slot 1 or 2 to neutralize spicy raccoon orders!'
    });
  }
  if (weights.tart && !typesPlaced.includes('vector_garden')) {
    alerts.push({
      kind: 'warn',
      text: 'Hallucination Warning! Berry Tarts need a Vector Berry Patch (RAG) so chefs do not guess facts!'
    });
  }
  if (weights.boba && !typesPlaced.includes('tool_blender')) {
    alerts.push({
      kind: 'warn',
      text: 'Math/API Alert! Add a Tool-Call Boba Mixer to run deterministic calculations accurately.'
    });
  }
  if (alerts.length === 0 && typesPlaced.length > 0) {
    alerts.push({
      kind: 'good',
      text: '5-Star AI Pipeline! Your kitchen has Guardrails, Grounding, and the right Model Ovens for this shift!'
    });
  }
  return alerts;
}


  4. Procedural Cozy Cafe WebAudio Synthesizer (audio.js)
Instead of bundling megabytes of external .mp3 assets, Prompt Paws synthesizes all cozy sound effects—station placement pops, upgrade arpeggios, cache-hit chimes, and guardrail blocks—using the browser's native Web Audio API:
class CozyCafeAudio {
  playTone(freq, type = 'sine', duration = 0.14, gainVal = 0.08, slideFreq = null) {
    if (!this.enabled) return;
    this.init();
    const now = this.ctx.currentTime;
    const osc = this.ctx.createOscillator();
    const gain = this.ctx.createGain();
    osc.type = type;
    osc.frequency.setValueAtTime(freq, now);
    if (slideFreq) {
      osc.frequency.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(slideFreq, now + duration);
    }
    gain.gain.setValueAtTime(gainVal, now);
    gain.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0.001, now + duration);
    osc.connect(gain);
    gain.connect(this.ctx.destination);
    osc.start(now);
    osc.stop(now + duration);
  }
  // Sparkly 4-note major arpeggio when upgrading an AI station
  playUpgrade() {
    [523.25, 659.25, 783.99, 1046.5].forEach((n, i) => {
      setTimeout(() => this.playTone(n, 'triangle', 0.14, 0.09), i * 55);
    });
  }
}
5. Try the 7-Day AI Engineering Challenge!
Can you beat all 7 Story Shifts—from Day 1 (Fast LLM Routing) all the way to Day 7 (Grand Festival Multi-Agent Banquet)—with a 0% Hallucination Rate and all 5 Cafe Hearts intact?
- Play in Browser: https://robinamirbahar.github.io/cozy-ai-agent-cafe-game/
